Essential Thrombocythaemia
Haematology & Oncology
Erythromelalgia, Unexplained venous/arterial thrombosis, Mild splenomegaly, Sustained thrombocytosis with JAK2/CALR mutation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-018
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of excruciating, burning pain and severe erythema in the bilateral feet accompanied by acute visual disturbances requiring urgent evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient experiencing an agonizing "burning fire" sensation in both feet that prevented standing, combined with a transient loss of vision in the right eye (amaurosis fugax) earlier in the evening. Urgent clinical evaluation was mandated to assess for acute microvascular occlusion or impending major arterial thrombosis, satisfying criteria for immediate after-hours intervention.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My feet are burning so badly I can't put on socks, they are bright red, and earlier tonight I went completely blind in my right eye for about ten minutes."
History of Present Illness: The 55-year-old patient reports an insidious onset of a chronic, unremitting headache and mild systemic malaise over the past two months. Tonight, they experienced the sudden onset of erythromelalgia—intense burning pain, throbbing, and severe erythema—in both feet. The symptoms are severely exacerbated by the warm room temperature. Furthermore, two hours ago, the patient experienced a sudden, painless visual disturbance, describing a "dark curtain" falling over their right eye (amaurosis fugax) that resolved spontaneously after 10 minutes. They also note recurrent, mild epistaxis over the past week.
Functional Status: Bedbound, keeping feet exposed to cool air due to severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high anxiety regarding the transient visual loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused adult. Visibly distressed by the foot pain.
Physical Exam:
Extremities: Bilateral feet and toes demonstrate severe erythema and feel intensely hot to the touch. Capillary refill is brisk. No distinct focal ulceration or gangrene. Peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis, posterior tibial) are palpable and bounding.
Neurological: Cranial nerves II-XII currently intact. Visual acuity grossly normal currently. No focal motor or sensory deficits.
Abdomen: Soft, non-tender. Mild, painless splenomegaly palpable 2 cm below the left costal margin.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No carotid bruits auscultated.
Environmental Observations: Patient has a fan blowing directly onto their bare feet.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient took 1g paracetamol with no effect on the burning pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Essential Thrombocythaemia - Status: Exacerbated (Erythromelalgia and TIA/Amaurosis Fugax)
Differentials:

Acute Peripheral Arterial Occlusion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the presence of bounding pulses, bright red (rather than pale/mottled) extremities, and the bilateral nature of the presentation.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the bilateral, strictly symmetrical presentation, absence of systemic fever, and the sudden, acute onset linked to temperature.

Diabetic Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the acute presentation of intense erythema and heat (erythromelalgia) rather than chronic numbness, and the crucial accompanying history of amaurosis fugax and splenomegaly.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The simultaneous presentation of severe erythromelalgia (microvascular occlusion in the extremities) and a transient ischaemic attack (amaurosis fugax) is highly characteristic of severe thrombocytosis, strongly suggesting undiagnosed Essential Thrombocythaemia. The palpable splenomegaly and recent epistaxis further support a myeloproliferative disorder. The transient visual loss represents a critical warning sign for an impending major stroke or retinal artery occlusion. Delaying assessment until the morning would expose the patient to the unacceptable risk of a catastrophic, irreversible cerebrovascular event overnight. The urgent home visit was essential to physically evaluate the neurological status, recognize the systemic microvascular crisis, and initiate immediate antiplatelet therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate oral Aspirin 300mg stat to rapidly inhibit platelet aggregation and treat the microvascular occlusion causing the erythromelalgia. Immediate referral to the Emergency Department for urgent neurological evaluation, full blood count (anticipating sustained thrombocytosis >450 x 10^9/L), and subsequent haematology review.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Keep your feet elevated and exposed to cool, moving air. By physically cooling the extremities, you constrict the superficial blood vessels, which directly reduces the severe burning pain of erythromelalgia.

Do not attempt to walk on the painful feet tonight. By avoiding pressure on the inflamed, congested blood vessels, you prevent localized tissue damage and reduce pain triggers.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department evaluation is required. Call 000 if your vision goes dark again, if you develop a severe sudden headache, or if you experience weakness on one side of your body.

